    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/9/49002.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 13,
    "turn_text": "And of course, that leads to then our other topic that we wanted to discuss today, which is the defund the police movement. So as you just very eloquently pointed out at the same time, we have those who are coming across our border, finding those gaps in the border, those who are selling drugs or here to do ill to us, you then have police systems that first of all may not have the resources to be able to handle the influx of crime in their cities, but also just feel like their hands are tied, even if they do arrest someone, they can get out on bail quickly. Tell me a little bit about the defund the police movement. At least in Florida, I know Florida hasn't seen it to the same extent we have seen it in other states. But just as somebody who works on Capitol Hill and hears about these issues on a day to day basis, how bad has the defund the police movement meant when it comes to crime? Because the Biden administration wants states because of the pandemic and guns we have increased crime.",
